{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-90c6e20844682b31", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tListen, Do Not Copy: Internalizing Audio-Grounded Scaffold Context\n2\tfor Robust Omni-Model Speech Understanding\n3\tPengfei Zhang1 , Biao Tian2 , Tianxin Xie1 , Minghao Yang1 , Xiangang Li2 , Li Liu1∗\n4\t1\n5\t\n6\tAI Thrust, Information Hub, The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology (Guangzhou)\n7\t2\n8\tToken Foundry, Alibaba Group\n9\tavrillliu@hkust-gz.edu.cn\n10\t\n11\tarXiv:2607.21943v1 [cs.SD] 24 Jul 2026\n12\t\n13\tAbstract\n14\tOmni models transcribe clean, single-speaker speech well,\n15\tbut their accuracy drops sharply when speakers overlap and\n16\tthe scene is noisy, exactly where knowing who said what\n17\tmatters most. A natural fix is a short scene description. We\n18\tshow why this is risky: answer-bearing text lets the model\n19\tcopy instead of listen, so the score rises although nothing has\n20\tbeen heard; a silent test exposes this shortcut at once. We\n21\tcall this failure mode perception bypass and address it with\n22\tAudio-Grounded Scaffold Context (AGSC). AGSC links\n23\tthree steps: first, we build clues from audio to guide listening\n24\twithout giving the answer; second, answer-overlap and silence\n25\ttests probe them for leakage and audio dependence; finally,\n26\tthose clues scaffold training but vanish at test time, yielding\n27\tno-clue capability. Across three heterogeneous Omni models,\n28\ttraining on AGSC lowers no-clue capped mean permutation\n29\tword error rate (mpWER) on overlapping, noisy speech from\n30\t25%–71% to 9%–15%. For streaming control, we formulate a\n31\tjoint GDPO task in which the model learns when to use a clue\n32\tand how to produce a speaker-attributed transcript from separately normalized format, gate, and transcript rewards. After\n33\tinternalization, AGSC adds almost no inference overhead.\n34\t\n35\tIntroduction\n36\tImagine a meeting recording in which two people briefly\n37\tspeak at once over the hum of an air conditioner. An Omni\n38\tmodel is a multimodal large language model that handles\n39\ttext, images, audio, and video in one backbone (Xu et al.\n40\t2025; Cui et al. 2026; Inclusion AI et al. 2025b). It may\n41\ttranscribe the clean parts well and then fail at the overlap: it\n42\tmerges two voices, drops a speaker, or treats noise as words.\n43\tThese conditions are common in meetings, call centers, incar assistants, and interviews, where knowing who said what\n44\tis hardest and most useful.\n45\tTextual scene descriptions seem like a natural aid when\n46\taudio is unclear. We tried the most direct version and supplied a full transcript as context. The mean accuracy of three\n47\tOmni models rose from about 53% to 94%. The jump was\n48\ttoo good to be true. On the primary-transcript task, the two\n49\tstronger models copied a wrong contextual answer 94%–\n50\t99.8% of the time; the weakest copied it 47% of the time.\n51\t∗\n52\t\n53\tCorresponding author.\n54\tCopyright © 2027, Association for the Advancement of Artificial\n55\tIntelligence (www.aaai.org). All rights reserved.\n56\t\n57\tWith silence in place of audio, all three still reached 100%\n58\tprimary-transcript accuracy. The high score measured copying, not better listening. We call this failure mode perception\n59\tbypass and turn it into a pass-or-fail test for context quality.\n60\tThis observation is our starting point. We hypothesize that\n61\tOmni models already have useful acoustic capabilities that\n62\tremain underused in complex scenes. Audio-Grounded Scaffold Context (AGSC) has two goals: draw out those capabilities and keep text from mimicking better listening. Its clues\n63\tgive only limited scene structure, and a silent-audio control\n64\tchecks whether the clue alone can still succeed without the\n65\taudio signal.\n66\tWe test this idea with AGSC (Figure 1). Instead of telling\n67\tthe model what was said, AGSC supplies three kinds of scaffolding: target-speaker activity, scene noise level, and partial,\n68\tshuffled words. Each clue should guide listening, not become\n69\ta text-only solution. To limit or expose context-only shortcuts, we use three safeguards: an audio-first instruction, an\n70\tanswer-overlap screen, and a silent-audio control. Standard tools build the clues from raw audio.\n71\tWe use two training paths so the learned behavior is not\n72\ttied to a fixed test-time prompt. Supervised fine-tuning (SFT)\n73\tmixes scaffolded and plain inputs; after training, we remove\n74\tclues and treat the retained no-clue gain as a behavioral definition of internalization. Group reward-decoupled normalization policy optimization (GDPO) (Liu et al. 2026) handles streaming control with separate rewards for output format, gate decisions, and transcript quality. Gate-only training\n75\tlearns whether and when to use a clue; full-chain training\n76\tlearns the gate and speaker-attributed transcript jointly. Both\n77\tpaths update low-rank adapters.\n78\tWe make three contributions. (1) We identify perception\n79\tbypass and turn silent audio into a behavioral design test\n80\tfor context. (2) We introduce AGSC (an automatic synthesis\n81\tpipeline) and Context-Speech Bench (CSB), which covers\n82\toverlap, noise, meetings, streaming clues, and gating. (3) We\n83\tshow internalization: supervised training improves no-clue\n84\ttranscription, and one GDPO stage jointly trains gate and\n85\ttranscript outputs with streaming clues from 160 streams.\n86\tWe report silent-audio controls beside the context results;\n87\tfollowing acceptance, we will open-source the AGSC code,\n88\tbenchmark manifests, and result evidence to support reproducibility and advance community research.\n89\t\n90\t\fFigure 1: Overview. (a) Answer-bearing context inflates scores by copying. (b) AGSC provides limited-content scaffold clues\n91\twith three safeguards. (c) After supervised training, capped mean permutation word error rate (mpWER) stays lower when the\n92\tclue is removed. The silent test is an aggregate control; we use the no-clue gain as operational evidence of internalization.\n93\t\n94\tRelated Work\n95\tAudio-language models join an audio encoder with a large\n96\tlanguage model. They range from Qwen-Audio (Chu et al.\n97\t2023) and SALMONN (Tang et al. 2024) to Omni systems\n98\tthat understand and generate several modalities (Xu et al.\n99\t2025; Cui et al. 2026; Inclusion AI et al. 2025a,b). Speakerattributed transcription remains difficult under overlap and\n100\tnoise (Kanda et al. 2020; Cornell et al. 2024). AIR-Bench,\n101\tMMAU, and Dynamic-SUPERB mainly test broad listening and reasoning ability (Yang et al. 2024; Sakshi et al.\n102\t2025; Huang et al. 2024). They do not directly test whether\n103\ttextual context leaks the answer. We add this control to\n104\tQwen3-Omni, MiniCPM-o, and Ming-flash-omni (henceforth Qwen3, MiniCPM, and Ming).\n105\tThe classical route chains diarization (Bredin 2023), voice\n106\tactivity detection (Silero Team 2024), automatic speech\n107\trecognition (ASR) (Radford et al. 2023), and sometimes separation (Subakan et al. 2021); early errors can spread. A second route trains one model to emit several speakers (Kanda\n108\tet al. 2020), uses speaker profiles (Medennikov et al. 2020),\n109\tor targets distant meetings (Cornell et al. 2024). We train\n110\tlow-rank adaptation (LoRA) modules on the attention projections and leave the pretrained weights unchanged (Hu et al.\n111\t2022). We reuse the front-end tools in a different role: they\n112\tproduce clues that the model must still check against audio,\n113\tnot answers it can copy. We keep a front-end component only\n114\twhen measurements show that it helps. Our focus is context\n115\tdesign and parameter-efficient adaptation of Omni models,\n116\tnot a separate transcription cascade.\n117\tRetrieval-augmented generation adds text from an external\n118\t\n119\tmemory (Lewis et al. 2020), while chain-of-thought prompting exposes intermediate reasoning steps (Wei et al. 2022).\n120\tContextual biasing gives a recognizer trusted word lists, such\n121\tas contact names (Pundak et al. 2018). Our candidate words\n122\tare different: they are automatic, possibly wrong, partial, and\n123\tshuffled. Text-only systems can also rewrite recognition or\n124\tdiarization outputs (Chen et al. 2023; Wang et al. 2024),\n125\tbut they cannot use the audio to correct a fluent but wrong\n126\tdraft. AGSC instead keeps audio as the source of the answer\n127\tand limits context to partial clues that the model must check\n128\tagainst the signal.\n129\tPerception bypass is a form of shortcut learning (Geirhos\n130\tet al. 2020). Our silent test has a role similar to languageprior controls used to rebalance visual question answering\n131\tdatasets (Goyal et al. 2017). Related failures also occur in\n132\tvision-language and audio-video models (Deng et al. 2025;\n133\tNishimura, Nakada, and Kondo 2024; Leng et al. 2025).\n134\tRecent work shows that large audio models can answer from\n135\ttext alone and uses that signal to split training data (He et al.\n136\t2026). We instead use silence as a design criterion and an\n137\tempirical control: context should fail without audio.\n138\tContext distillation moves the benefit of instructions or\n139\tscratchpads into model weights (Snell, Klein, and Zhong\n140\t2022). We ask a related question: does clue-conditioned\n141\ttraining improve no-clue evaluation, and can a model learn\n142\twhen a clue would be useful? Interference-Resilient Adaptive Fusion (IRAF) studies a related problem in full-duplex\n143\tdialogue, where an interfering speaker enters the user microphone (Zhong et al. 2026). It predicts input reliability and\n144\tsuppresses an unreliable signal. Both IRAF and our method\n145\t\n146\t\fuse a learned gate, but at different points. IRAF reduces interference before fusion; we keep every speaker and gate\n147\ta limited-content text clue. Its continuous gate provides a\n148\tuseful comparison with our discrete clue gate.\n149\t\n150\tMethod\n151\tAGSC has three linked steps: it synthesizes answerincomplete clues from audio, screens them for direct answer\n152\texposure and audio dependence, and uses them as removable training scaffolds. A separate GDPO study learns a joint\n153\tgate-plus-transcript output for streaming control.\n154\t\n155\tProblem and Perception Bypass\n156\tGiven an audio clip a with K speakers, the model must output one line per speaker. Let y = (y1 , . . . , yK ) be the gold\n157\ttranscriptions and ŷ the predicted lines. We score outputs\n158\twith mean permutation word error rate (mpWER), a speakeraveraged variant of concatenated minimum-permutation\n159\tword error rate (cpWER) (Watanabe et al. 2020). It is\n160\tthe equally weighted mean of per-speaker word error rates\n161\t(WER) under the best speaker permutation (lower is better):\n162\t\u0001\n163\tK\n164\t1 X ED yk , ŷρ(k)\n165\tmpWER(y, ŷ) = min\n166\t(1)\n167\tρ∈SK K\n168\t|yk |\n169\tk=1\n170\t\n171\tIn Equation (1), ρ ∈ SK assigns lines to reference speakers,\n172\tED is word-level edit distance, and |yk | is reference length.\n173\tMissing output lines are padded as empty. For this twospeaker benchmark, including streaming evaluation, we score\n174\tonly the first two output lines. Let c be a textual context. We\n175\tseek a context whose gain depends on a, not on c alone.\n176\tAn overly informative c can make a irrelevant; this is\n177\tperception bypass. The introduction showed that a transcriptbearing context inflates accuracy by copying: the answer is\n178\ta copyable substring, wrong labels are copied, and silent\n179\taudio still scores perfectly. We turn this finding into a design\n180\tcriterion. For model M , a clue c is desirable for sample (a, y)\n181\tonly when it cannot solve the sample without audio:\n182\t\u0001\n183\tmpWER y, M (a∅ , c) ≥ η\n184\t(2)\n185\twhere a∅ is a silent track of the same length as a. We set\n186\tη = 1 (an mpWER of at least 1) for transcription; for classification probes, we report accuracy and use chance level\n187\tas the analogous boundary. Equation (2) states the desired\n188\tsample-level property. At evaluation, we apply the same idea\n189\tat the aggregate level by measuring performance after the\n190\taudio is silenced. This is an empirical control, not a proof\n191\tthat every shortcut is absent.\n192\t\n193\tAGSC: Clues, Not Answers\n194\tThis rule leads directly to a simple design principle: a clue\n195\tmay point and narrow, but it must not disclose the answer.\n196\tAGSC applies this principle to each acoustic scene.\n197\tFor overlap, AGSC provides a time window for each\n198\tspeaker, such as target active 0.00–3.85 s, female, but no\n199\twords. For single-speaker noise, it provides a coarse noise\n200\tlevel, speech regions, and a partial, shuffled word list. We\n201\tkeep about two thirds of the candidate words and remove\n202\t\n203\texact answers such as digits. For overlap plus noise, a separator produces a rough draft for each voice. AGSC keeps\n204\thalf of the content words, adds up to four distractors from\n205\tother recordings, removes duplicates, and shuffles the result.\n206\tIt never shows the full draft. Figure 1(b) shows this format.\n207\tMissing words, distractors, and lost word order reduce directly copyable content.\n208\tThe clue type matches the uncertainty. A time window addresses who and when; coarse attributes describe the listening\n209\tcondition; and a lossy word set narrows lexical alternatives.\n210\tRemoving sentence order, completeness, and exact-value tokens keeps the clue directional rather than answer-like. An\n211\toverlap-plus-noise clue therefore labels its candidates as partial, shuffled, and possibly wrong, omits much of the draft,\n212\tand never presents a complete sentence.\n213\tThree safeguards limit or expose shortcuts. The audiofirst instruction marks every clue as automatic and fallible.\n214\tThe answer-overlap screen rejects a directly copyable answer string, while the silent-audio control tests whether the\n215\tremaining clue can still replace sound. Clean audio does not\n216\tneed a clue, so a gate adds one only for detected overlap or\n217\tlow signal-to-noise ratio (SNR). The gate handles a separate\n218\trisk: a non-answer clue can still distract a segment that needs\n219\tno help.\n220\t\n221\tAutomatic Synthesis Pipeline\n222\tThe automatic pipeline turns raw audio into AGSC. It diarizes speakers (Bredin 2023), runs segment ASR to obtain\n223\tcandidate words, and detects overlap, speech regions (Silero\n224\tTeam 2024), and a coarse SNR level. For overlap plus noise,\n225\tSepFormer separates the voices (Subakan et al. 2021); perspeaker ASR then supplies words for shuffling. The gate\n226\tdecides whether to add a clue, and the exporter records the\n227\tclue with a confidence tag.\n228\tEach component must earn its place through measurement.\n229\tWe select the diarizer with lower diarization error rate (DER,\n230\t0.22 versus 0.47) and remove an acoustic-scene tagger with\n231\tonly 29% accuracy. SNR is useful only as a coarse level: that\n232\tlevel is correct 79% of the time, although the point estimate\n233\thas 9.9 dB mean error. On twelve evaluation recordings, the\n234\tfull pipeline reaches DER 0.15, finds the correct speaker\n235\tcount for 83% of clips, and obtains draft character error\n236\trate (CER) 0.20. The confidence tag indicates how much the\n237\tmodel should trust the front end rather than presenting an\n238\tuncertain clue as ground truth.\n239\t\n240\tSupervised Training and No-Clue Evaluation\n241\tThe key test is whether a post-training improvement remains\n242\tafter the clue text is removed. We perform SFT with LoRA on\n243\tthe attention projections (rank 16, α=32). For the trainable\n244\tLoRA parameters θ, we minimize the usual next-token loss\n245\tover the serialized per-speaker target ȳ,\n246\tLSFT (θ) = −\n247\t\n248\t|ȳ|\n249\tX\n250\t\n251\t\u0001\n252\tlog pθ ȳt | ȳ<t , a, c\n253\t\n254\t(3)\n255\t\n256\tt=1\n257\t\n258\tEquation (3) uses the gated AGSC clue as c when the front\n259\tend flags the segment and an empty context otherwise. This\n260\t\n261\t\fmixture includes both scaffolded and plain inputs. After training, the no-clue condition asks whether the behavior persists\n262\tafter the auxiliary text is removed. All runs use AdamW\n263\t(learning rate 1.5 × 10−4 ) for one epoch; backbone-specific\n264\texecution details and shared settings are given in the supplement.\n265\t\n266\tGDPO for Gating and Streaming Outputs\n267\tFigure 2 summarizes the full-chain and gate-only GDPO\n268\tsettings used below.\n269\tOutput format, gate choice, and transcript quality are\n270\twhole-output signals, so we train them with rewards rather\n271\tthan imitate a fixed answer. Group relative policy optimization (GRPO) (Shao et al. 2024) sums the rewards before\n272\tgroup normalization; in our setting, this can hide different\n273\treward patterns. GDPO instead scales each reward j within\n274\tthe rollout group i = 1, . . . , G (Liu et al. 2026),\n275\tX\n276\trj,i − µj\n277\tÂj,i =\n278\t,\n279\tAi =\n280\twj Âj,i\n281\t(4)\n282\tσj + δ\n283\tj\n284\twith µj , σj the group mean and standard deviation of reward\n285\tj, δ = 10−6 , and default w = (1, 1, 2) over (format, gate,\n286\tASR); a batch-level renormalization of Ai stabilizes training.\n287\tThe balanced variant (v2) raises the gate weight to w =\n288\t(1, 2, 2). It also uses the conditional reward below. Writing u\n289\tfor the gate token the model emits and u⋆ for the scene truth,\n290\tthe three rewards are\n291\trfmt = ⊮[gate present ∧ body nonempty],\n292\trgate = ⊮[u = u⋆ ] rfmt ,\n293\t\u0001\n294\trasr = 1 − min(1, mpWER) rfmt\n295\t\n296\t(5)\n297\t\n298\tso a malformed output earns nothing. A conditional reward\n299\tpays an easy reward only once a hard one is met,\n300\treasy ← reasy ·⊮[ rhard ≥ τ ],\n301\t\n302\there rasr ← rasr ·rgate (6)\n303\t\n304\twhere τ is the pass threshold of the harder reward (the binary\n305\tgate reward, so τ = 1), preventing the policy from earning\n306\ttranscription reward while ignoring the gate.\n307\tThe balanced objective therefore has an explicit hierarchy.\n308\tA valid format exposes the decision, a correct gate opens\n309\ttranscription credit, and mpWER then ranks the content.\n310\tSeparate normalization keeps the signal from each level visible instead of allowing one reward to hide the others.\n311\tGroup-relative training stalls when a confident policy samples G identical actions, giving zero variance and zero gradient. For the binary gating decision we reduce this risk by\n312\tcomputing the policy exactly,\n313\tpθ (sequ )\n314\tπ(u) = P\n315\t,\n316\tv pθ (seqv )\n317\t\n318\tu, v ∈ {complex, clean}\n319\t\n320\t(7)\n321\t\n322\tHere sequ is the gate line for u. We compute it exactly with\n323\ttwo forward passes per window and sample from a mixture\n324\tbehavior policy,\n325\tπb = (1 − ε) π + ε Uniform,\n326\t\n327\tε = 0.25\n328\t\n329\t(8)\n330\t\n331\tEquations (7) and (8) define the binary target policy and\n332\tits exploratory behavior policy. The mixture keeps both\n333\t\n334\tactions possible and reduces, but does not remove, the\n335\tchance of a zero-variance group. We use the importance\n336\tratio π(u)/πb (u) in a clipped proximal policy optimization\n337\t(PPO)-style update to partially compensate for the behaviorpolicy mismatch (Schulman et al. 2017). This analytic policy\n338\tis used only in the separate gate-only study, not in the fullchain rollout.\n339\tEach full-chain episode is one of 120 three-part composites (clean, then overlap plus noise, then clean; 75%) or 40\n340\tpure-clean streams (25%). Each episode is paired with a clue\n341\tof random maturity (none, or built from the first 2 s, the first\n342\t4 s, or the full stream). In a single generation, the model emits\n343\ta gate line followed by per-speaker transcription. Thus the\n344\tgate token and transcript are trained jointly while the clue\n345\tis available (Figure 2). The mpWER in Equation (5) uses\n346\tclean-segment pseudo-labels plus overlap gold. The gate reward uses scene truth from the composite construction.\n347\tIn the gate-only study, a pre-measured downstream reward\n348\treplaces the transcription reward,\n349\t\n350\t⋆\n351\ttanh(∆s /0.2) u = u = complex,\n352\trdown = −0.5\n353\tu = complex, u⋆ = clean,\n354\t\n355\t0\n356\tu = clean\n357\t(9)\n358\tIn Equation (9), ∆s is the measured mpWER benefit of injecting the clue on stream s (clipped at zero), so the loop\n359\tneeds no extra transcription pass. In both reinforcement settings, Qwen3 uses LoRA (rank, α) = (16, 32), whereas\n360\tMiniCPM and Ming use (8, 16). All runs use AdamW\n361\twith gradient-norm clip 1. The full chain uses learning rate\n362\t4 × 10−5 , G=4 rollouts, and temperature 1.1; the gate-only\n363\tstudy uses learning rate 2 × 10−5 , G=8, and PPO ratio clip\n364\t0.2. Further details appear in the supplement.\n365\t\n366\tExperiments\n367\tWe test three Omni models: Qwen3-Omni 30B (Xu et al.\n368\t2025), MiniCPM-o 4.5 (9B) (Cui et al. 2026), and Mingflash-omni 2.0, a 100B-total, 6.1B-active-per-token mixtureof-experts model (Inclusion AI et al. 2025b).\n369\tCSB covers overlap, noise, meetings, streaming clue maturity, and gating. Its English speech comes from LibriSpeech\n370\tand SparseLibriMix2 (Panayotov et al. 2015; Cosentino et al.\n371\t2020); Mandarin comes from AISHELL-3 mixes (Shi et al.\n372\t2021); noise comes from WHAM and the Music, Speech,\n373\tand Noise (MUSAN) corpus (Wichern et al. 2019; Snyder,\n374\tChen, and Povey 2015); and meetings come from the Augmented Multi-party Interaction (AMI) Meeting Corpus (Carletta et al. 2006). Gold answers and predicted clues are stored\n375\tseparately. CSB contains 13,255 training and 1,525 evaluation samples. It also has dedicated splits for gate-only reinforcement (120 streams; 240 paired windows), full-chain reinforcement (160 streams), clue maturity (385 prefix clues),\n376\tand real-time gating (30 streams). The supplement gives the\n377\tsplit table and construction details.\n378\tWe report mpWER, Mandarin CER, and gating F1 (the\n379\tharmonic mean of precision and recall). For each hardestthird summary, we select the subset once from the pretraining no-clue baseline and reuse it across conditions; these\n380\t\n381\t\fFigure 2: GDPO settings. (a) Full-chain training emits one gate-plus-transcript output from a stream-like composite and a\n382\trandom-maturity clue. Rewards are normalized separately; balanced v2 conditions ASR credit on the correct gate. (b) Gate-only\n383\ttraining samples from an ε-mixture policy πb around an exact target policy π and uses π/πb in the clipped update.\n384\tsummaries are descriptive. The supervised and full-chain results cap the mpWER of each sample at 1 before averaging,\n385\twhile streaming diagnostics keep their recorded raw means.\n386\tWe report the available 95% confidence intervals (CIs) for\n387\tthe full-set comparisons.\n388\tThe experiments follow four questions: does answerbearing context replace listening; does scaffold-conditioned\n389\ttraining improve transcription after the clue is removed; how\n390\tdoes clue utility change with prefix maturity; and can reinforcement learning train the gate and full chain? A targeted\n391\tAMI case between the first two questions illustrates how a\n392\twordless clue can guide target selection. This order separates shortcut detection, no-clue transfer, streaming utility,\n393\tand learned control.\n394\tThe first question is whether perception bypass occurs\n395\t(Table 1). The answer is clear. On the primary-transcript\n396\ttask, blind-copy rates reach 99.8% with a wrong answer in\n397\tcontext, and accuracy remains 100% on silent audio. The\n398\tinflated scores therefore measure copying. Ming copies less\n399\toften (47%) mainly because it fails to follow the answer\n400\tformat, not because it listens better. All three models fail this\n401\tcontrol. By contrast, in the gated-AGSC silence control on\n402\tthe transcription and speaker-counting probes, the aggregate\n403\ttask score is 0% when audio is replaced by silence but the\n404\toriginal clue is kept. This is consistent with less context-only\n405\tcopying on these probes.\n406\tBefore the training studies, we inspect a target-selection\n407\tcase on real AMI meetings. The unclued Qwen3 output can\n408\tinclude speech beyond the target, consistent with targetlocation ambiguity. A predicted time window contains no\n409\ttranscript words; it narrows where to listen while leaving\n410\t\n411\tModel\n412\tQwen3\n413\tMiniCPM\n414\tMing\n415\t\n416\tNo context +Answer Blind-copy\n417\t65.6\n418\t53.4\n419\t40.1\n420\t\n421\t93.3\n422\t96.8\n423\t91.1\n424\t\n425\t94.2\n426\t99.8\n427\t47.0\n428\t\n429\tTable 1: Perception bypass. The first two columns are\n430\tcomposite task scores (%); blind-copy is measured on the\n431\tprimary-transcript task under a wrong contextual answer.\n432\tThe copied answers show that the inflated score does not\n433\testablish better listening.\n434\tword recovery to audio. This illustrates the intended division\n435\tof labor, not a claim of uniform test-time gain.\n436\tThis case also shows why a clue must match the scene. A\n437\ttime window addresses who and when; under noise, partial\n438\tand shuffled candidates narrow lexical uncertainty without\n439\tbecoming a second transcript. The three evaluations therefore\n440\tanswer different questions: no-clue transcription asks what\n441\tremains after scaffold-conditioned training; the prefix study\n442\tasks when a runtime clue becomes useful while the heard\n443\taudio stays fixed; and joint training asks whether the learned\n444\tgate-plus-transcript output is accurate.\n445\tThe second question is whether the post-training skill persists without a clue. Performance improves after AGSC finetuning even with no clue at test time on the 150-sample\n446\toverlap-plus-noise evaluation split (Figure 3, Table 2). On\n447\tthis split, mpWER falls from 25%–71% to 9%–15%. On the\n448\thardest-third summaries, it falls from 50%–96% to 13%–\n449\t17%. We use this persistence after clue removal as operational evidence of internalization.\n450\tThe weaker models gain more: Ming drops by 56 points,\n451\t\n452\t\fFigure 3: Supervised internalization on overlap plus noise\n453\t(n=150; right: the fixed, base-defined hardest 50 of each\n454\tmodel). Gray is pre-training; green is post-training with no\n455\tclue. Capped mean mpWER falls across all three models;\n456\tunder our operational definition, this no-clue improvement\n457\tis internalization.\n458\t\n459\tFigure 4: Prefix-built clues for the pre-SFT base models on\n460\tthe fixed, base-defined hardest 18 samples of each model.\n461\tThe model hears the full utterance; only the clue uses the\n462\tindicated prefix. Curves show raw mpWER gain over no\n463\tclue; lower ticks give mean clue keywords over all 55 clips.\n464\tMing reaches 93% of its terminal gain at 6 s.\n465\t\n466\tMiniCPM by 52, and Qwen3 by 16. All three end in a narrow\n467\t9–15 band. The same pattern holds for overlap without added\n468\tnoise, where mpWER falls from 26.6, 64.1, and 68.3 to 8.4,\n469\t14.9, and 7.9 for Qwen3, MiniCPM, and Ming, respectively.\n470\tThe effect is therefore not tied to one test condition.\n471\tFor MiniCPM, an always-on test-time clue after training\n472\tlags the no-clue result (22.5 versus 14.0). During training,\n473\tclues arrive only through the gate, so the model sees them\n474\tonly for selected segments. Adding one unconditionally at\n475\ttest time therefore changes the input distribution and can\n476\thurt accuracy.\n477\tThe full set and fixed hardest third answer different questions. The full set measures average behavior. The hardest\n478\tthird shows where baseline errors concentrate and remains\n479\tdescriptive. The full-set no-clue CIs provide the uncertainty\n480\tcheck for the internalization claim. The prefix study below\n481\tmeasures runtime clue utility while holding the heard audio\n482\tfixed throughout.\n483\tThe third question asks how clue utility changes with the\n484\tclue-builder prefix (Figure 4). The full utterance is always\n485\theard; only the clue uses that prefix, and every curve uses the\n486\tpre-SFT base model. Each curve reports raw mpWER gain\n487\ton the fixed, base-defined hardest 18 of that model. At 1 s,\n488\tthe zero-keyword clue still includes the instruction and an\n489\t\n490\tempty frame. Results are non-monotonic: Ming gains 15.1\n491\tpoints at 2 s and 16.7 at 6 s, or 93% of its 18.0-point terminal\n492\tgain. MiniCPM peaks at 8 s; for Qwen3, the terminal clue\n493\thurts this hard slice (−2.3 points).\n494\tThe external signal detector reduces clue insertions by\n495\t65% by adding clues only to detected complex segments. A\n496\tnaive time-window clue lowers Qwen3 clean-segment recall\n497\tfrom 97.6% to 73.5%. The instruction still transcribe the\n498\tentire stream restores it to 97.6% and gives the best streaming\n499\tmpWER. Streaming therefore needs enough prefix audio to\n500\tbuild a useful clue and a gate that protects the rest of the\n501\tstream from needless clues.\n502\tThe fourth question is whether reinforcement learning can\n503\ttrain streaming control. We start with the gate alone. In the\n504\tmatched MiniCPM comparison, gate-only GDPO reaches the\n505\t0.875 correct-action ceiling imposed by ε=0.25 exploration.\n506\tGRPO stops at 0.75–0.80 after an early collapse (supplement). This stage learns only detection and timing: complex\n507\trecall rises to 0.70–1.0, while post-training clean recall is\n508\t0.80–0.90 across models.\n509\tAs a real-time detector, the learned gate reaches framelevel timeline F1 0.753–0.803, compared with 0.53 for the\n510\texternal detector on the same 30-stream split. The external\n511\tdetector triggers at 1.72 s; after GDPO, the learned gates\n512\ttrigger at about 0.2 s across models (Table 3). Gate-only\n513\ttraining predicts whether and when a clue would be useful; it\n514\tdoes not train transcription. On a separate speaker-counting\n515\tprobe, accuracy improves by 10 percentage points (pp) for\n516\tQwen3 and 6.2 pp for Ming, and is unchanged for MiniCPM.\n517\tThe full-chain study optimizes one gate-plus-speakerattributed-transcript output. It uses streaming clues from only\n518\t160 streams, compared with the 3,500 noise-stage examples\n519\tused for the supervised runs. With no clue at test time, mpWER drops by 14.1 points for MiniCPM and 64.2 for Ming;\n520\tthe reported CIs for both changes exclude zero (Table 4).\n521\tThe Qwen3 drop is 2.8 points and its reported CI [−1.0, 7.2]\n522\tincludes zero; Qwen3 starts as the strongest no-clue transcriber (33.0), so it has the least headroom. Ming starts with\n523\t\n524\tModel\n525\t\n526\tNo clue\n527\tClue at test Hardest 1/3\n528\t(pre→post) (pre→post) (pre→post)\n529\t\n530\tQwen3\n531\t24.7→9.2 26.4→9.1 49.9→16.8\n532\tMiniCPM 65.8→14.0 62.6→22.5 86.3→12.8\n533\tMing\n534\t71.1→15.0 72.1→14.7 96.4→14.9\n535\t\n536\tTable 2: Evaluation mpWER (↓; per-sample cap 1) before/after supervised training with AGSC examples (n=150).\n537\tThe Clue at test column uses an unconditional test-time clue;\n538\tHardest 1/3 reports the no-clue condition and is descriptive. The 95% CIs for full-set no-clue drops are [12.1, 19.0],\n539\t[47.9, 55.7], and [51.4, 60.8] points.\n540\t\n541\t\fModel\n542\t\n543\tcomplex\n544\trecall\n545\t\n546\tF1\n547\t(timeline)\n548\t\n549\tLatency\n550\t(s)\n551\t\n552\tSpeaker\n553\tcounting\n554\t\n555\tQwen3\n556\t0.567→1.00 0.727→0.803 0.73→0.20 16.3→26.3\n557\tMiniCPM 0.567→0.70 0.720→0.769 0.53→0.17 35.0→35.0\n558\tMing\n559\t0.20→1.00 0.522→0.753 1.19→0.23 15.0→21.2\n560\t\n561\tTable 3: Gate-only GDPO (pre→post). Complex recall\n562\tuses 30 complex windows; F1 /latency use 30 streams;\n563\tspeaker counting uses a separate 80-example probe. External\n564\tF1 /latency are 0.53/1.72 s; Table 4 gives full-chain mpWER.\n565\tModel\n566\t\n567\tNo clue 2 s clue 4 s clue Full clue\n568\t\n569\tQwen3 base\n570\tQwen3 chain\n571\tMiniCPM base\n572\tMiniCPM chain v1\n573\tMing base\n574\tMing chain\n575\t\n576\t33.0\n577\t30.2\n578\t58.5\n579\t44.4\n580\t100.0\n581\t35.8\n582\t\n583\t31.9\n584\t29.6\n585\t56.1\n586\t49.6\n587\t80.6\n588\t33.6\n589\t\n590\t33.7\n591\t27.9\n592\t58.7\n593\t50.9\n594\t83.7\n595\t32.2\n596\t\n597\t31.6\n598\t29.9\n599\t57.0\n600\t42.9\n601\t89.9\n602\t38.4\n603\t\n604\tTable 4: Full-chain capped mean mpWER (↓; per-sample\n605\tcap 1; n=55 per condition/model). No-clue drop 95% CIs:\n606\tQwen3 [−1.0, 7.2], MiniCPM [7.1, 21.9], Ming [57.1, 70.9].\n607\tthe highest no-clue mpWER (100.0) and therefore has the\n608\tmost headroom.\n609\tMiniCPM exposes a conflict: a heavy transcription reward\n610\tsharply lowers complex-gate recall. Table 4 reports this v1\n611\tcheckpoint. In balanced v2, we set w = (1, 2, 2) in Equation (4) and condition transcription credit on a correct gate\n612\tusing Equation (6). Complex-gate recall recovers above baseline while no-clue mpWER becomes 55.5 (supplement).\n613\tHeavy training can cause gate collapse: the model reports\n614\tcomplex for almost every clean stream (clean-stream accuracy 0, 0, and 0.11). A forced-choice probe shows a median\n615\tcomplex probability of 1.00 for both stream types, so changing a soft threshold cannot help. At this extreme, a logit gap\n616\tnear 20 drives both the per-token gradient p(1−p), with p\n617\tthe gate probability, and the group advantage toward zero.\n618\tA short supervised step provides a strong gradient in this\n619\tstate. After 90 steps, clean-stream gate accuracy returns to\n620\t1.0 for all models; macro-averaged mixed-stream mpWER\n621\talso decreases (supplement). The runs in Table 4 stop before\n622\tthis regime; the collapse appears only under heavier training.\n623\tGate-only GDPO predicts clue use and timing, measured\n624\tby recall, timeline F1 , and latency; speaker counting uses\n625\ta separate probe. Its ε-mixture keeps both actions possible\n626\tand reduces zero-variance groups. Full-chain GDPO jointly\n627\ttrains gate and transcript, with mpWER scoring only the\n628\ttranscript. It separately normalizes format, gate, and transcript rewards, and balanced v2 blocks transcript credit after\n629\ta wrong gate.\n630\tAGSC adds 113–157 prompt tokens, but audio processing dominates inference. Wall-clock overhead is 0.03–0.21 s\n631\t(2.9–11.9%) except for Ming on overlap plus noise, where\n632\ttask completion produces an apparent 69% increase; see the\n633\tsupplement for the protocol. Clues are built offline in 1.56 s\n634\tand can be cached. No-clue evaluation uses the plain prompt\n635\twithout clue construction or clue tokens.\n636\t\n637\tTwo deployment paths follow. Streaming builds a clue\n638\tfrom prefix audio and uses the learned gate to decide when\n639\tto inject it, paying clue-building and prompt overhead. The\n640\tno-clue path uses the plain audio prompt without the clue\n641\tbuilder. Gate metrics evaluate the first path; no-clue mpWER\n642\tevaluates the second.\n643\t\n644\tDiscussion\n645\tOur hypothesis is simple: some complex-scene failures reflect an underused capability, not an absent one. AGSC tries\n646\tto draw out that capability without turning its clues into\n647\ttextual shortcuts. During SFT, an incomplete target window,\n648\tcondition tag, or candidate list acts as a temporary trigger. We\n649\tcall the lower error that remains after removing the trigger retained activation. Answer-overlap screening limits copying;\n650\tthe silent-audio control measures performance after silencing the audio.\n651\tEach check answers a different question: context-on evaluation asks whether a clue helps; silence asks whether it\n652\tcan replace audio; no-clue evaluation asks whether behavior\n653\tpersists after scaffold removal; and streaming metrics ask\n654\twhether assistance is timed correctly. No check substitutes\n655\tfor another. We use internalization for post-training persistence without clues.\n656\tSFT targets persistence after scaffold removal; GDPO separates gate-only timing from full-chain gate-plus-transcript\n657\ttraining under separately normalized rewards.\n658\tHow useful a clue is depends on the scene and on how\n659\tmuch prefix audio was available to build it. A wordless window can narrow target location, while Figure 4 shows nonmonotonic, model-specific changes as prefix evidence grows.\n660\tTable 2 also shows that an unconditional clue after training\n661\tneed not beat the no-clue path. There is no universal prompt.\n662\tOur predefined templates match clue type to the diagnosed\n663\tambiguity, and the gate limits when a clue enters.\n664\tTogether, the results separate what persists without runtime help (no-clue mpWER), when a clue should enter (gate\n665\tmetrics), and transcript accuracy under joint training (fullchain mpWER). Runtime clues remain in the streaming path,\n666\tkeeping assistance optional and failures visible.\n667\t\n668\tConclusion\n669\tThis paper turns a tempting shortcut into a design principle.\n670\tAnswer-bearing context can let a model score without listening; we call this perception bypass and expose it by silencing audio. That test anchors three contributions: AGSC uses\n671\tanswer-incomplete scaffolds to guide listening while limiting direct copying; CSB evaluates them with explicit shortcut\n672\tcontrols; and internalization is judged by whether gains persist after clues are removed. After SFT, no-clue capped mean\n673\tmpWER on overlap plus noise falls from 25%–71% to 9%–\n674\t15%, providing operational evidence of internalization. For\n675\tstreaming, gate-only GDPO learns whether and when to use\n676\ta clue; full-chain GDPO jointly trains the gate and speakerattributed transcript. Future work will extend scene-matched\n677\tclues to new scene types and test the learned gate under domain shift. The deeper principle is simple: context should\n678\tscaffold perception, not replace it.\n679\t\n680\t\fReferences\n681\tBredin, H. 2023. pyannote.audio 2.1 speaker diarization\n682\tpipeline: principle, benchmark, and recipe. In Proceedings\n683\tof the Annual Conference of the International Speech Communication Association (INTERSPEECH), 1983–1987.\n684\tCarletta, J.; Ashby, S.; Bourban, S.; Flynn, M.; Guillemot,\n685\tM.; Hain, T.; Kadlec, J.; Karaiskos, V.; Kraaij, W.; Kronenthal, M.; Lathoud, G.; Lincoln, M.; Lisowska, A.; McCowan,\n686\tI.; Post, W.; Reidsma, D.; and Wellner, P. 2006. The AMI\n687\tMeeting Corpus: A Pre-announcement. In Machine Learning\n688\tfor Multimodal Interaction, Second International Workshop,\n689\tvolume 3869 of Lecture Notes in Computer Science, 28–39.\n690\tSpringer.\n691\tChen, C.; Hu, Y.; Yang, C.-H. H.; Siniscalchi, S. M.; Chen,\n692\tP.-Y.; and Chng, E. S. 2023. HyPoradise: An Open Baseline for Generative Speech Recognition with Large Language\n693\tModels. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, Datasets and Benchmarks Track (NeurIPS), volume 36,\n694\t31665–31688.\n695\tChu, Y.; Xu, J.; Zhou, X.; Yang, Q.; Zhang, S.; Yan, Z.;\n696\tZhou, C.; and Zhou, J. 2023. Qwen-Audio: Advancing Universal Audio Understanding via Unified Large-Scale AudioLanguage Models. arXiv:2311.07919.\n697\tCornell, S.; Park, T. J.; Huang, H.; Boeddeker, C.; Chang, X.;\n698\tMaciejewski, M.; Wiesner, M. S.; Garcia, P.; and Watanabe,\n699\tS. 2024. The CHiME-8 DASR Challenge for Generalizable and Array Agnostic Distant Automatic Speech Recognition and Diarization. In Proceedings of the International\n700\tWorkshop on Speech Processing in Everyday Environments\n701\t(CHiME), 1–6.\n702\tCosentino, J.; Pariente, M.; Cornell, S.; Deleforge, A.; and\n703\tVincent, E. 2020. LibriMix: An Open-Source Dataset for\n704\tGeneralizable Speech Separation. arXiv:2005.11262.\n705\tCui, J.; Xu, B.; Wang, C.; Yu, T.; Sun, W.; et al. 2026.\n706\tMiniCPM-o 4.5: Towards Real-Time Full-Duplex OmniModal Interaction. arXiv:2604.27393.\n707\tDeng, A.; Cao, T.; Chen, Z.; and Hooi, B. 2025. Words\n708\tor Vision: Do Vision-Language Models Have Blind Faith\n709\tin Text? In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on\n710\tComputer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR), 3867–\n711\t3876.\n712\tGeirhos, R.; Jacobsen, J.-H.; Michaelis, C.; Zemel, R.; Brendel, W.; Bethge, M.; and Wichmann, F. A. 2020. Shortcut\n713\tLearning in Deep Neural Networks. Nature Machine Intelligence, 2(11): 665–673.\n714\tGoyal, Y.; Khot, T.; Summers-Stay, D.; Batra, D.; and Parikh,\n715\tD. 2017. Making the V in VQA Matter: Elevating the Role\n716\tof Image Understanding in Visual Question Answering. In\n717\tProceedings of the IEEE Conference on Computer Vision\n718\tand Pattern Recognition (CVPR), 6904–6913.\n719\tHe, H.; Du, X.; Sun, R.; Dai, Z.; Xiao, Y.; Yang, M.; Zhou, J.;\n720\tLi, X.; Liu, Z.; Liang, Z.; Wu, C.; He, Q.; Lee, T.; Chen, X.;\n721\tZheng, W.-L.; Wang, W.; Plumbley, M. D.; Liu, J.; and Kong,\n722\tQ. 2026. Measuring Audio’s Impact on Correctness: AudioContribution-Aware Post-Training of Large Audio Language\n723\tModels. In Proceedings of the International Conference on\n724\tLearning Representations (ICLR).\n725\t\n726\tHu, E. J.; Shen, Y.; Wallis, P.; Allen-Zhu, Z.; Li, Y.; Wang, S.;\n727\tWang, L.; and Chen, W. 2022. LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation\n728\tof Large Language Models. In Proceedings of the International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR).\n729\tHuang, C.-y.; Lu, K.-H.; Wang, S.-H.; Hsiao, C.-Y.; Kuan, C.Y.; Wu, H.; Arora, S.; Chang, K.-W.; Shi, J.; Peng, Y.; Sharma,\n730\tR.; Watanabe, S.; Ramakrishnan, B.; Shehata, S.; and Lee,\n731\tH.-y. 2024. Dynamic-SUPERB: Towards a Dynamic, Collaborative, and Comprehensive Instruction-Tuning Benchmark\n732\tfor Speech. In Proceedings of the IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP),\n733\t12136–12140.\n734\tInclusion AI; Gong, B.; Zou, C.; Zheng, C.; Zhou, C.; Yan,\n735\tC.; et al. 2025a. Ming-Omni: A Unified Multimodal Model\n736\tfor Perception and Generation. arXiv:2506.09344.\n737\tInclusion AI; Ma, B.; Zou, C.; Du, C.; Yan, C.; Jin,\n738\tC.; et al. 2025b. Ming-Flash-Omni: A Sparse, Unified\n739\tArchitecture for Multimodal Perception and Generation.\n740\tarXiv:2510.24821.\n741\tKanda, N.; Gaur, Y.; Wang, X.; Meng, Z.; and Yoshioka, T.\n742\t2020. Serialized Output Training for End-to-End Overlapped\n743\tSpeech Recognition. In Proceedings of the Annual Conference of the International Speech Communication Association\n744\t(INTERSPEECH), 2797–2801.\n745\tLeng, S.; Xing, Y.; Cheng, Z.; Zhou, Y.; Zhang, H.; Li, X.;\n746\tZhao, D.; Lu, S.; Miao, C.; and Bing, L. 2025. The Curse of\n747\tMulti-Modalities: Evaluating Hallucinations of Large Multimodal Models across Language, Visual, and Audio. In\n748\tBelgrave, D.; Zhang, C.; Lin, H.; Pascanu, R.; Koniusz, P.;\n749\tGhassemi, M.; and Chen, N., eds., Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, volume 38. Curran Associates,\n750\tInc.\n751\tLewis, P.; Perez, E.; Piktus, A.; et al. 2020. RetrievalAugmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks.\n752\tIn Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems\n753\t(NeurIPS), volume 33, 9459–9474.\n754\tLiu, S.-Y.; Dong, X.; Lu, X.; Diao, S.; Belcak, P.; Liu, M.;\n755\tChen, M.-H.; Yin, H. D.; Wang, Y.-C.; Cheng, K.-T.; Choi,\n756\tY.; Kautz, J.; and Molchanov, P. 2026. GDPO: Group rewardDecoupled Normalization Policy Optimization for Multireward RL Optimization. In Proceedings of the 43rd International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML).\n757\tMedennikov, I.; Korenevsky, M.; Prisyach, T.; Khokhlov, Y.;\n758\tKorenevskaya, M.; Sorokin, I.; Timofeeva, T.; Mitrofanov,\n759\tA.; Andrusenko, A.; Podluzhny, I.; Laptev, A.; and Romanenko, A. 2020. Target-Speaker Voice Activity Detection: A\n760\tNovel Approach for Multi-Speaker Diarization in a Dinner\n761\tParty Scenario. In Proceedings of the Annual Conference of\n762\tthe International Speech Communication Association (INTERSPEECH), 274–278.\n763\tNishimura, T.; Nakada, S.; and Kondo, M. 2024. On the Audio Hallucinations in Large Audio-Video Language Models.\n764\tarXiv:2401.09774.\n765\tPanayotov, V.; Chen, G.; Povey, D.; and Khudanpur, S. 2015.\n766\tLibriSpeech: An ASR Corpus Based on Public Domain Audio Books. In Proceedings of the IEEE International Confer-\n767\t\n768\t\fence on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP),\n769\t5206–5210.\n770\tPundak, G.; Sainath, T. N.; Prabhavalkar, R.; Kannan, A.;\n771\tand Zhao, D. 2018. Deep Context: End-to-End Contextual\n772\tSpeech Recognition. In Proceedings of the IEEE Spoken\n773\tLanguage Technology Workshop (SLT), 418–425.\n774\tRadford, A.; Kim, J. W.; Xu, T.; Brockman, G.; McLeavey,\n775\tC.; and Sutskever, I. 2023. Robust Speech Recognition via\n776\tLarge-Scale Weak Supervision. In Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML), volume\n777\t202, 28492–28518. PMLR.\n778\tSakshi, S.; Tyagi, U.; Kumar, S.; Seth, A.; Selvakumar, R.;\n779\tNieto, O.; Duraiswami, R.; Ghosh, S.; and Manocha, D.\n780\t2025. MMAU: A Massive Multi-Task Audio Understanding\n781\tand Reasoning Benchmark. In Yue, Y.; Garg, A.; Peng,\n782\tN.; Sha, F.; and Yu, R., eds., International Conference on\n783\tLearning Representations, volume 2025, 84929–84964.\n784\tSchulman, J.; Wolski, F.; Dhariwal, P.; Radford, A.; and\n785\tKlimov, O. 2017. Proximal Policy Optimization Algorithms.\n786\tarXiv:1707.06347.\n787\tShao, Z.; Wang, P.; Zhu, Q.; Xu, R.; Song, J.; et al. 2024.\n788\tDeepSeekMath: Pushing the Limits of Mathematical Reasoning in Open Language Models. arXiv:2402.03300.\n789\tShi, Y.; Bu, H.; Xu, X.; Zhang, S.; and Li, M. 2021.\n790\tAISHELL-3: A Multi-Speaker Mandarin TTS Corpus.\n791\tIn Proceedings of the Annual Conference of the International Speech Communication Association (INTERSPEECH), 2756–2760.\n792\tSilero Team. 2024. Silero VAD: Pre-trained EnterpriseGrade Voice Activity Detector (VAD), Number Detector and\n793\tLanguage Classifier. https://github.com/snakers4/silero-vad.\n794\tSnell, C.; Klein, D.; and Zhong, R. 2022. Learning by Distilling Context. arXiv:2209.15189.\n795\tSnyder, D.; Chen, G.; and Povey, D. 2015. MUSAN: A\n796\tMusic, Speech, and Noise Corpus. arXiv:1510.08484.\n797\tSubakan, C.; Ravanelli, M.; Cornell, S.; Bronzi, M.; and\n798\tZhong, J. 2021. Attention Is All You Need in Speech Separation. In Proceedings of the IEEE International Conference\n799\ton Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), 21–\n800\t25.\n801\tTang, C.; Yu, W.; Sun, G.; Chen, X.; et al. 2024. SALMONN:\n802\tTowards Generic Hearing Abilities for Large Language Models. In Proceedings of the International Conference on\n803\tLearning Representations (ICLR).\n804\tWang, Q.; Huang, Y.; Zhao, G.; Clark, E.; Xia, W.; and\n805\tLiao, H. 2024. DiarizationLM: Speaker Diarization PostProcessing with Large Language Models. In Proceedings of\n806\tthe Annual Conference of the International Speech Communication Association (INTERSPEECH), 3754–3758.\n807\tWatanabe, S.; Mandel, M.; Barker, J.; Vincent, E.; et al. 2020.\n808\tCHiME-6 Challenge: Tackling Multispeaker Speech Recognition for Unsegmented Recordings. In Proceedings of the\n809\tInternational Workshop on Speech Processing in Everyday\n810\tEnvironments (CHiME), 1–7.\n811\tWei, J.; Wang, X.; Schuurmans, D.; et al. 2022. Chainof-Thought Prompting Elicits Reasoning in Large Language\n812\t\n813\tModels. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS), volume 35, 24824–24837.\n814\tWichern, G.; Antognini, J.; Flynn, M.; Zhu, L. R.; et al.\n815\t2019. WHAM!: Extending Speech Separation to Noisy Environments. In Proceedings of the Annual Conference of the\n816\tInternational Speech Communication Association (INTERSPEECH), 1368–1372.\n817\tXu, J.; Guo, Z.; Hu, H.; Chu, Y.; Wang, X.; et al. 2025.\n818\tQwen3-Omni Technical Report. arXiv:2509.17765.\n819\tYang, Q.; Xu, J.; Liu, W.; Chu, Y.; Jiang, Z.; Zhou, X.; Leng,\n820\tY.; Lv, Y.; Zhao, Z.; Zhou, C.; and Zhou, J. 2024. AIR-Bench:\n821\tBenchmarking Large Audio-Language Models via Generative Comprehension. In Proceedings of the 62nd Annual\n822\tMeeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics\n823\t(Volume 1: Long Papers), 1979–1998.\n824\tZhong, T.; Deng, J.; Kuzmin, N.; Zhu, Y.; Cao, T.; Tsoi,\n825\tT.; Tan, Z.; Lui, S.; and Liu, X. 2026. IRAF: InterferenceResilient Adaptive Fusion for Noise-Robust End-to-End FullDuplex Spoken Dialogue Systems. arXiv:2606.06559.\n826\t\n827\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "6b4e9d6d-d69d-4845-8e88-eed229957829", "uuid": "5294ec3e-a13c-422e-98f3-1ec437db073c", "timestamp": "2026-07-27T06:07:51.799Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2607_21943.txt", "content": "Listen, Do Not Copy: Internalizing Audio-Grounded Scaffold Context\nfor Robust Omni-Model Speech Understanding\nPengfei Zhang1 , Biao Tian2 , Tianxin Xie1 , Minghao Yang1 , Xiangang Li2 , Li Liu1∗\n1\n\nAI Thrust, Information Hub, The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology (Guangzhou)\n2\nToken Foundry, Alibaba Group\navrillliu@hkust-gz.edu.cn\n\narXiv:2607.21943v1 [cs.SD] 24 Jul 2026\n\nAbstract\nOmni models transcribe clean, single-speaker speech well,\nbut their accuracy drops sharply when speakers overlap and\nthe scene is noisy, exactly where knowing who said what\nmatters most. A natural fix is a short scene description. We\nshow why this is risky: answer-bearing text lets the model\ncopy instead of listen, so the score rises although nothing has\nbeen heard; a silent test exposes this shortcut at once. We\ncall this failure mode perception bypass and address it with\nAudio-Grounded Scaffold Context (AGSC). AGSC links\nthree steps: first, we build clues from audio to guide listening\nwithout giving the answer; second, answer-overlap and silence\ntests probe them for leakage and audio dependence; finally,\nthose clues scaffold training but vanish at test time, yielding\nno-clue capability. Across three heterogeneous Omni models,\ntraining on AGSC lowers no-clue capped mean permutation\nword error rate (mpWER) on overlapping, noisy speech from\n25%–71% to 9%–15%. For streaming control, we formulate a\njoint GDPO task in which the model learns when to use a clue\nand how to produce a speaker-attributed transcript from separately normalized format, gate, and transcript rewards. After\ninternalization, AGSC adds almost no inference overhead.\n\nIntroduction\nImagine a meeting recording in which two people briefly\nspeak at once over the hum of an air conditioner. An Omni\nmodel is a multimodal large language model that handles\ntext, images, audio, and video in one backbone (Xu et al.\n2025; Cui et al. 2026; Inclusion AI et al. 2025b). It may\ntranscribe the clean parts well and then fail at the overlap: it\nmerges two voices, drops a speaker, or treats noise as words.\nThese conditions are common in meetings, call centers, incar assistants, and interviews, where knowing who said what\nis hardest and most useful.\nTextual scene descriptions seem like a natural aid when\naudio is unclear. We tried the most direct version and supplied a full transcript as context. The mean accuracy of three\nOmni models rose from about 53% to 94%. The jump was\ntoo good to be true. On the primary-transcript task, the two\nstronger models copied a wrong contextual answer 94%–\n99.8% of the time; the weakest copied it 47% of the time.\n∗\n\nCorresponding author.\nCopyright © 2027, Association for the Advancement of Artificial\nIntelligence (www.aaai.org). All rights reserved.\n\nWith silence in place of audio, all three still reached 100%\nprimary-transcript accuracy. The high score measured copying, not better listening. We call this failure mode perception\nbypass and turn it into a pass-or-fail test for context quality.\nThis observation is our starting point. We hypothesize that\nOmni models already have useful acoustic capabilities that\nremain underused in complex scenes. Audio-Grounded Scaffold Context (AGSC) has two goals: draw out those capabilities and keep text from mimicking better listening. Its clues\ngive only limited scene structure, and a silent-audio control\nchecks whether the clue alone can still succeed without the\naudio signal.\nWe test this idea with AGSC (Figure 1). Instead of telling\nthe model what was said, AGSC supplies three kinds of scaffolding: target-speaker activity, scene noise level, and partial,\nshuffled words. Each clue should guide listening, not become\na text-only solution. To limit or expose context-only shortcuts, we use three safeguards: an audio-first instruction, an\nanswer-overlap screen, and a silent-audio control. Standard tools build the clues from raw audio.\nWe use two training paths so the learned behavior is not\ntied to a fixed test-time prompt. Supervised fine-tuning (SFT)\nmixes scaffolded and plain inputs; after training, we remove\nclues and treat the retained no-clue gain as a behavioral definition of internalization. Group reward-decoupled normalization policy optimization (GDPO) (Liu et al. 2026) handles streaming control with separate rewards for output format, gate decisions, and transcript quality. Gate-only training\nlearns whether and when to use a clue; full-chain training\nlearns the gate and speaker-attributed transcript jointly. Both\npaths update low-rank adapters.\nWe make three contributions. (1) We identify perception\nbypass and turn silent audio into a behavioral design test\nfor context. (2) We introduce AGSC (an automatic synthesis\npipeline) and Context-Speech Bench (CSB), which covers\noverlap, noise, meetings, streaming clues, and gating. (3) We\nshow internalization: supervised training improves no-clue\ntranscription, and one GDPO stage jointly trains gate and\ntranscript outputs with streaming clues from 160 streams.\nWe report silent-audio controls beside the context results;\nfollowing acceptance, we will open-source the AGSC code,\nbenchmark manifests, and result evidence to support reproducibility and advance community research.\n\n\fFigure 1: Overview. (a) Answer-bearing context inflates scores by copying. (b) AGSC provides limited-content scaffold clues\nwith three safeguards. (c) After supervised training, capped mean permutation word error rate (mpWER) stays lower when the\nclue is removed. The silent test is an aggregate control; we use the no-clue gain as operational evidence of internalization.\n\nRelated Work\nAudio-language models join an audio encoder with a large\nlanguage model. They range from Qwen-Audio (Chu et al.\n2023) and SALMONN (Tang et al. 2024) to Omni systems\nthat understand and generate several modalities (Xu et al.\n2025; Cui et al. 2026; Inclusion AI et al. 2025a,b). Speakerattributed transcription remains difficult under overlap and\nnoise (Kanda et al. 2020; Cornell et al. 2024). AIR-Bench,\nMMAU, and Dynamic-SUPERB mainly test broad listening and reasoning ability (Yang et al. 2024; Sakshi et al.\n2025; Huang et al. 2024). They do not directly test whether\ntextual context leaks the answer. We add this control to\nQwen3-Omni, MiniCPM-o, and Ming-flash-omni (henceforth Qwen3, MiniCPM, and Ming).\nThe classical route chains diarization (Bredin 2023), voice\nactivity detection (Silero Team 2024), automatic speech\nrecognition (ASR) (Radford et al. 2023), and sometimes separation (Subakan et al. 2021); early errors can spread. A second route trains one model to emit several speakers (Kanda\net al. 2020), uses speaker profiles (Medennikov et al. 2020),\nor targets distant meetings (Cornell et al. 2024). We train\nlow-rank adaptation (LoRA) modules on the attention projections and leave the pretrained weights unchanged (Hu et al.\n2022). We reuse the front-end tools in a different role: they\nproduce clues that the model must still check against audio,\nnot answers it can copy. We keep a front-end component only\nwhen measurements show that it helps. Our focus is context\ndesign and parameter-efficient adaptation of Omni models,\nnot a separate transcription cascade.\nRetrieval-augmented generation adds text from an external\n\nmemory (Lewis et al. 2020), while chain-of-thought prompting exposes intermediate reasoning steps (Wei et al. 2022).\nContextual biasing gives a recognizer trusted word lists, such\nas contact names (Pundak et al. 2018). Our candidate words\nare different: they are automatic, possibly wrong, partial, and\nshuffled. Text-only systems can also rewrite recognition or\ndiarization outputs (Chen et al. 2023; Wang et al. 2024),\nbut they cannot use the audio to correct a fluent but wrong\ndraft. AGSC instead keeps audio as the source of the answer\nand limits context to partial clues that the model must check\nagainst the signal.\nPerception bypass is a form of shortcut learning (Geirhos\net al. 2020). Our silent test has a role similar to languageprior controls used to rebalance visual question answering\ndatasets (Goyal et al. 2017). Related failures also occur in\nvision-language and audio-video models (Deng et al. 2025;\nNishimura, Nakada, and Kondo 2024; Leng et al. 2025).\nRecent work shows that large audio models can answer from\ntext alone and uses that signal to split training data (He et al.\n2026). We instead use silence as a design criterion and an\nempirical control: context should fail without audio.\nContext distillation moves the benefit of instructions or\nscratchpads into model weights (Snell, Klein, and Zhong\n2022). We ask a related question: does clue-conditioned\ntraining improve no-clue evaluation, and can a model learn\nwhen a clue would be useful? Interference-Resilient Adaptive Fusion (IRAF) studies a related problem in full-duplex\ndialogue, where an interfering speaker enters the user microphone (Zhong et al. 2026). It predicts input reliability and\nsuppresses an unreliable signal. Both IRAF and our method\n\n\fuse a learned gate, but at different points. IRAF reduces interference before fusion; we keep every speaker and gate\na limited-content text clue. Its continuous gate provides a\nuseful comparison with our discrete clue gate.\n\nMethod\nAGSC has three linked steps: it synthesizes answerincomplete clues from audio, screens them for direct answer\nexposure and audio dependence, and uses them as removable training scaffolds. A separate GDPO study learns a joint\ngate-plus-transcript output for streaming control.\n\nProblem and Perception Bypass\nGiven an audio clip a with K speakers, the model must output one line per speaker. Let y = (y1 , . . . , yK ) be the gold\ntranscriptions and ŷ the predicted lines. We score outputs\nwith mean permutation word error rate (mpWER), a speakeraveraged variant of concatenated minimum-permutation\nword error rate (cpWER) (Watanabe et al. 2020). It is\nthe equally weighted mean of per-speaker word error rates\n(WER) under the best speaker permutation (lower is better):\n\u0001\nK\n1 X ED yk , ŷρ(k)\nmpWER(y, ŷ) = min\n(1)\nρ∈SK K\n|yk |\nk=1\n\nIn Equation (1), ρ ∈ SK assigns lines to reference speakers,\nED is word-level edit distance, and |yk | is reference length.\nMissing output lines are padded as empty. For this twospeaker benchmark, including streaming evaluation, we score\nonly the first two output lines. Let c be a textual context. We\nseek a context whose gain depends on a, not on c alone.\nAn overly informative c can make a irrelevant; this is\nperception bypass. The introduction showed that a transcriptbearing context inflates accuracy by copying: the answer is\na copyable substring, wrong labels are copied, and silent\naudio still scores perfectly. We turn this finding into a design\ncriterion. For model M , a clue c is desirable for sample (a, y)\nonly when it cannot solve the sample without audio:\n\u0001\nmpWER y, M (a∅ , c) ≥ η\n(2)\nwhere a∅ is a silent track of the same length as a. We set\nη = 1 (an mpWER of at least 1) for transcription; for classification probes, we report accuracy and use chance level\nas the analogous boundary. Equation (2) states the desired\nsample-level property. At evaluation, we apply the same idea\nat the aggregate level by measuring performance after the\naudio is silenced. This is an empirical control, not a proof\nthat every shortcut is absent.\n\nAGSC: Clues, Not Answers\nThis rule leads directly to a simple design principle: a clue\nmay point and narrow, but it must not disclose the answer.\nAGSC applies this principle to each acoustic scene.\nFor overlap, AGSC provides a time window for each\nspeaker, such as target active 0.00–3.85 s, female, but no\nwords. For single-speaker noise, it provides a coarse noise\nlevel, speech regions, and a partial, shuffled word list. We\nkeep about two thirds of the candidate words and remove\n\nexact answers such as digits. For overlap plus noise, a separator produces a rough draft for each voice. AGSC keeps\nhalf of the content words, adds up to four distractors from\nother recordings, removes duplicates, and shuffles the result.\nIt never shows the full draft. Figure 1(b) shows this format.\nMissing words, distractors, and lost word order reduce directly copyable content.\nThe clue type matches the uncertainty. A time window addresses who and when; coarse attributes describe the listening\ncondition; and a lossy word set narrows lexical alternatives.\nRemoving sentence order, completeness, and exact-value tokens keeps the clue directional rather than answer-like. An\noverlap-plus-noise clue therefore labels its candidates as partial, shuffled, and possibly wrong, omits much of the draft,\nand never presents a complete sentence.\nThree safeguards limit or expose shortcuts. The audiofirst instruction marks every clue as automatic and fallible.\nThe answer-overlap screen rejects a directly copyable answer string, while the silent-audio control tests whether the\nremaining clue can still replace sound. Clean audio does not\nneed a clue, so a gate adds one only for detected overlap or\nlow signal-to-noise ratio (SNR). The gate handles a separate\nrisk: a non-answer clue can still distract a segment that needs\nno help.\n\nAutomatic Synthesis Pipeline\nThe automatic pipeline turns raw audio into AGSC. It diarizes speakers (Bredin 2023), runs segment ASR to obtain\ncandidate words, and detects overlap, speech regions (Silero\nTeam 2024), and a coarse SNR level. For overlap plus noise,\nSepFormer separates the voices (Subakan et al. 2021); perspeaker ASR then supplies words for shuffling. The gate\ndecides whether to add a clue, and the exporter records the\nclue with a confidence tag.\nEach component must earn its place through measurement.\nWe select the diarizer with lower diarization error rate (DER,\n0.22 versus 0.47) and remove an acoustic-scene tagger with\nonly 29% accuracy. SNR is useful only as a coarse level: that\nlevel is correct 79% of the time, although the point estimate\nhas 9.9 dB mean error. On twelve evaluation recordings, the\nfull pipeline reaches DER 0.15, finds the correct speaker\ncount for 83% of clips, and obtains draft character error\nrate (CER) 0.20. The confidence tag indicates how much the\nmodel should trust the front end rather than presenting an\nuncertain clue as ground truth.\n\nSupervised Training and No-Clue Evaluation\nThe key test is whether a post-training improvement remains\nafter the clue text is removed. We perform SFT with LoRA on\nthe attention projections (rank 16, α=32). For the trainable\nLoRA parameters θ, we minimize the usual next-token loss\nover the serialized per-speaker target ȳ,\nLSFT (θ) = −\n\n|ȳ|\nX\n\n\u0001\nlog pθ ȳt | ȳ<t , a, c\n\n(3)\n\nt=1\n\nEquation (3) uses the gated AGSC clue as c when the front\nend flags the segment and an empty context otherwise. This\n\n\fmixture includes both scaffolded and plain inputs. After training, the no-clue condition asks whether the behavior persists\nafter the auxiliary text is removed. All runs use AdamW\n(learning rate 1.5 × 10−4 ) for one epoch; backbone-specific\nexecution details and shared settings are given in the supplement.\n\nGDPO for Gating and Streaming Outputs\nFigure 2 summarizes the full-chain and gate-only GDPO\nsettings used below.\nOutput format, gate choice, and transcript quality are\nwhole-output signals, so we train them with rewards rather\nthan imitate a fixed answer. Group relative policy optimization (GRPO) (Shao et al. 2024) sums the rewards before\ngroup normalization; in our setting, this can hide different\nreward patterns. GDPO instead scales each reward j within\nthe rollout group i = 1, . . . , G (Liu et al. 2026),\nX\nrj,i − µj\nÂj,i =\n,\nAi =\nwj Âj,i\n(4)\nσj + δ\nj\nwith µj , σj the group mean and standard deviation of reward\nj, δ = 10−6 , and default w = (1, 1, 2) over (format, gate,\nASR); a batch-level renormalization of Ai stabilizes training.\nThe balanced variant (v2) raises the gate weight to w =\n(1, 2, 2). It also uses the conditional reward below. Writing u\nfor the gate token the model emits and u⋆ for the scene truth,\nthe three rewards are\nrfmt = ⊮[gate present ∧ body nonempty],\nrgate = ⊮[u = u⋆ ] rfmt ,\n\u0001\nrasr = 1 − min(1, mpWER) rfmt\n\n(5)\n\nso a malformed output earns nothing. A conditional reward\npays an easy reward only once a hard one is met,\nreasy ← reasy ·⊮[ rhard ≥ τ ],\n\nhere rasr ← rasr ·rgate (6)\n\nwhere τ is the pass threshold of the harder reward (the binary\ngate reward, so τ = 1), preventing the policy from earning\ntranscription reward while ignoring the gate.\nThe balanced objective therefore has an explicit hierarchy.\nA valid format exposes the decision, a correct gate opens\ntranscription credit, and mpWER then ranks the content.\nSeparate normalization keeps the signal from each level visible instead of allowing one reward to hide the others.\nGroup-relative training stalls when a confident policy samples G identical actions, giving zero variance and zero gradient. For the binary gating decision we reduce this risk by\ncomputing the policy exactly,\npθ (sequ )\nπ(u) = P\n,\nv pθ (seqv )\n\nu, v ∈ {complex, clean}\n\n(7)\n\nHere sequ is the gate line for u. We compute it exactly with\ntwo forward passes per window and sample from a mixture\nbehavior policy,\nπb = (1 − ε) π + ε Uniform,\n\nε = 0.25\n\n(8)\n\nEquations (7) and (8) define the binary target policy and\nits exploratory behavior policy. The mixture keeps both\n\nactions possible and reduces, but does not remove, the\nchance of a zero-variance group. We use the importance\nratio π(u)/πb (u) in a clipped proximal policy optimization\n(PPO)-style update to partially compensate for the behaviorpolicy mismatch (Schulman et al. 2017). This analytic policy\nis used only in the separate gate-only study, not in the fullchain rollout.\nEach full-chain episode is one of 120 three-part composites (clean, then overlap plus noise, then clean; 75%) or 40\npure-clean streams (25%). Each episode is paired with a clue\nof random maturity (none, or built from the first 2 s, the first\n4 s, or the full stream). In a single generation, the model emits\na gate line followed by per-speaker transcription. Thus the\ngate token and transcript are trained jointly while the clue\nis available (Figure 2). The mpWER in Equation (5) uses\nclean-segment pseudo-labels plus overlap gold. The gate reward uses scene truth from the composite construction.\nIn the gate-only study, a pre-measured downstream reward\nreplaces the transcription reward,\n\n⋆\ntanh(∆s /0.2) u = u = complex,\nrdown = −0.5\nu = complex, u⋆ = clean,\n\n0\nu = clean\n(9)\nIn Equation (9), ∆s is the measured mpWER benefit of injecting the clue on stream s (clipped at zero), so the loop\nneeds no extra transcription pass. In both reinforcement settings, Qwen3 uses LoRA (rank, α) = (16, 32), whereas\nMiniCPM and Ming use (8, 16). All runs use AdamW\nwith gradient-norm clip 1. The full chain uses learning rate\n4 × 10−5 , G=4 rollouts, and temperature 1.1; the gate-only\nstudy uses learning rate 2 × 10−5 , G=8, and PPO ratio clip\n0.2. Further details appear in the supplement.\n\nExperiments\nWe test three Omni models: Qwen3-Omni 30B (Xu et al.\n2025), MiniCPM-o 4.5 (9B) (Cui et al. 2026), and Mingflash-omni 2.0, a 100B-total, 6.1B-active-per-token mixtureof-experts model (Inclusion AI et al. 2025b).\nCSB covers overlap, noise, meetings, streaming clue maturity, and gating. Its English speech comes from LibriSpeech\nand SparseLibriMix2 (Panayotov et al. 2015; Cosentino et al.\n2020); Mandarin comes from AISHELL-3 mixes (Shi et al.\n2021); noise comes from WHAM and the Music, Speech,\nand Noise (MUSAN) corpus (Wichern et al. 2019; Snyder,\nChen, and Povey 2015); and meetings come from the Augmented Multi-party Interaction (AMI) Meeting Corpus (Carletta et al. 2006). Gold answers and predicted clues are stored\nseparately. CSB contains 13,255 training and 1,525 evaluation samples. It also has dedicated splits for gate-only reinforcement (120 streams; 240 paired windows), full-chain reinforcement (160 streams), clue maturity (385 prefix clues),\nand real-time gating (30 streams). The supplement gives the\nsplit table and construction details.\nWe report mpWER, Mandarin CER, and gating F1 (the\nharmonic mean of precision and recall). For each hardestthird summary, we select the subset once from the pretraining no-clue baseline and reuse it across conditions; these\n\n\fFigure 2: GDPO settings. (a) Full-chain training emits one gate-plus-transcript output from a stream-like composite and a\nrandom-maturity clue. Rewards are normalized separately; balanced v2 conditions ASR credit on the correct gate. (b) Gate-only\ntraining samples from an ε-mixture policy πb around an exact target policy π and uses π/πb in the clipped update.\nsummaries are descriptive. The supervised and full-chain results cap the mpWER of each sample at 1 before averaging,\nwhile streaming diagnostics keep their recorded raw means.\nWe report the available 95% confidence intervals (CIs) for\nthe full-set comparisons.\nThe experiments follow four questions: does answerbearing context replace listening; does scaffold-conditioned\ntraining improve transcription after the clue is removed; how\ndoes clue utility change with prefix maturity; and can reinforcement learning train the gate and full chain? A targeted\nAMI case between the first two questions illustrates how a\nwordless clue can guide target selection. This order separates shortcut detection, no-clue transfer, streaming utility,\nand learned control.\nThe first question is whether perception bypass occurs\n(Table 1). The answer is clear. On the primary-transcript\ntask, blind-copy rates reach 99.8% with a wrong answer in\ncontext, and accuracy remains 100% on silent audio. The\ninflated scores therefore measure copying. Ming copies less\noften (47%) mainly because it fails to follow the answer\nformat, not because it listens better. All three models fail this\ncontrol. By contrast, in the gated-AGSC silence control on\nthe transcription and speaker-counting probes, the aggregate\ntask score is 0% when audio is replaced by silence but the\noriginal clue is kept. This is consistent with less context-only\ncopying on these probes.\nBefore the training studies, we inspect a target-selection\ncase on real AMI meetings. The unclued Qwen3 output can\ninclude speech beyond the target, consistent with targetlocation ambiguity. A predicted time window contains no\ntranscript words; it narrows where to listen while leaving\n\nModel\nQwen3\nMiniCPM\nMing\n\nNo context +Answer Blind-copy\n65.6\n53.4\n40.1\n\n93.3\n96.8\n91.1\n\n94.2\n99.8\n47.0\n\nTable 1: Perception bypass. The first two columns are\ncomposite task scores (%); blind-copy is measured on the\nprimary-transcript task under a wrong contextual answer.\nThe copied answers show that the inflated score does not\nestablish better listening.\nword recovery to audio. This illustrates the intended division\nof labor, not a claim of uniform test-time gain.\nThis case also shows why a clue must match the scene. A\ntime window addresses who and when; under noise, partial\nand shuffled candidates narrow lexical uncertainty without\nbecoming a second transcript. The three evaluations therefore\nanswer different questions: no-clue transcription asks what\nremains after scaffold-conditioned training; the prefix study\nasks when a runtime clue becomes useful while the heard\naudio stays fixed; and joint training asks whether the learned\ngate-plus-transcript output is accurate.\nThe second question is whether the post-training skill persists without a clue. Performance improves after AGSC finetuning even with no clue at test time on the 150-sample\noverlap-plus-noise evaluation split (Figure 3, Table 2). On\nthis split, mpWER falls from 25%–71% to 9%–15%. On the\nhardest-third summaries, it falls from 50%–96% to 13%–\n17%. We use this persistence after clue removal as operational evidence of internalization.\nThe weaker models gain more: Ming drops by 56 points,\n\n\fFigure 3: Supervised internalization on overlap plus noise\n(n=150; right: the fixed, base-defined hardest 50 of each\nmodel). Gray is pre-training; green is post-training with no\nclue. Capped mean mpWER falls across all three models;\nunder our operational definition, this no-clue improvement\nis internalization.\n\nFigure 4: Prefix-built clues for the pre-SFT base models on\nthe fixed, base-defined hardest 18 samples of each model.\nThe model hears the full utterance; only the clue uses the\nindicated prefix. Curves show raw mpWER gain over no\nclue; lower ticks give mean clue keywords over all 55 clips.\nMing reaches 93% of its terminal gain at 6 s.\n\nMiniCPM by 52, and Qwen3 by 16. All three end in a narrow\n9–15 band. The same pattern holds for overlap without added\nnoise, where mpWER falls from 26.6, 64.1, and 68.3 to 8.4,\n14.9, and 7.9 for Qwen3, MiniCPM, and Ming, respectively.\nThe effect is therefore not tied to one test condition.\nFor MiniCPM, an always-on test-time clue after training\nlags the no-clue result (22.5 versus 14.0). During training,\nclues arrive only through the gate, so the model sees them\nonly for selected segments. Adding one unconditionally at\ntest time therefore changes the input distribution and can\nhurt accuracy.\nThe full set and fixed hardest third answer different questions. The full set measures average behavior. The hardest\nthird shows where baseline errors concentrate and remains\ndescriptive. The full-set no-clue CIs provide the uncertainty\ncheck for the internalization claim. The prefix study below\nmeasures runtime clue utility while holding the heard audio\nfixed throughout.\nThe third question asks how clue utility changes with the\nclue-builder prefix (Figure 4). The full utterance is always\nheard; only the clue uses that prefix, and every curve uses the\npre-SFT base model. Each curve reports raw mpWER gain\non the fixed, base-defined hardest 18 of that model. At 1 s,\nthe zero-keyword clue still includes the instruction and an\n\nempty frame. Results are non-monotonic: Ming gains 15.1\npoints at 2 s and 16.7 at 6 s, or 93% of its 18.0-point terminal\ngain. MiniCPM peaks at 8 s; for Qwen3, the terminal clue\nhurts this hard slice (−2.3 points).\nThe external signal detector reduces clue insertions by\n65% by adding clues only to detected complex segments. A\nnaive time-window clue lowers Qwen3 clean-segment recall\nfrom 97.6% to 73.5%. The instruction still transcribe the\nentire stream restores it to 97.6% and gives the best streaming\nmpWER. Streaming therefore needs enough prefix audio to\nbuild a useful clue and a gate that protects the rest of the\nstream from needless clues.\nThe fourth question is whether reinforcement learning can\ntrain streaming control. We start with the gate alone. In the\nmatched MiniCPM comparison, gate-only GDPO reaches the\n0.875 correct-action ceiling imposed by ε=0.25 exploration.\nGRPO stops at 0.75–0.80 after an early collapse (supplement). This stage learns only detection and timing: complex\nrecall rises to 0.70–1.0, while post-training clean recall is\n0.80–0.90 across models.\nAs a real-time detector, the learned gate reaches framelevel timeline F1 0.753–0.803, compared with 0.53 for the\nexternal detector on the same 30-stream split. The external\ndetector triggers at 1.72 s; after GDPO, the learned gates\ntrigger at about 0.2 s across models (Table 3). Gate-only\ntraining predicts whether and when a clue would be useful; it\ndoes not train transcription. On a separate speaker-counting\nprobe, accuracy improves by 10 percentage points (pp) for\nQwen3 and 6.2 pp for Ming, and is unchanged for MiniCPM.\nThe full-chain study optimizes one gate-plus-speakerattributed-transcript output. It uses streaming clues from only\n160 streams, compared with the 3,500 noise-stage examples\nused for the supervised runs. With no clue at test time, mpWER drops by 14.1 points for MiniCPM and 64.2 for Ming;\nthe reported CIs for both changes exclude zero (Table 4).\nThe Qwen3 drop is 2.8 points and its reported CI [−1.0, 7.2]\nincludes zero; Qwen3 starts as the strongest no-clue transcriber (33.0), so it has the least headroom. Ming starts with\n\nModel\n\nNo clue\nClue at test Hardest 1/3\n(pre→post) (pre→post) (pre→post)\n\nQwen3\n24.7→9.2 26.4→9.1 49.9→16.8\nMiniCPM 65.8→14.0 62.6→22.5 86.3→12.8\nMing\n71.1→15.0 72.1→14.7 96.4→14.9\n\nTable 2: Evaluation mpWER (↓; per-sample cap 1) before/after supervised training with AGSC examples (n=150).\nThe Clue at test column uses an unconditional test-time clue;\nHardest 1/3 reports the no-clue condition and is descriptive. The 95% CIs for full-set no-clue drops are [12.1, 19.0],\n[47.9, 55.7], and [51.4, 60.8] points.\n\n\fModel\n\ncomplex\nrecall\n\nF1\n(timeline)\n\nLatency\n(s)\n\nSpeaker\ncounting\n\nQwen3\n0.567→1.00 0.727→0.803 0.73→0.20 16.3→26.3\nMiniCPM 0.567→0.70 0.720→0.769 0.53→0.17 35.0→35.0\nMing\n0.20→1.00 0.522→0.753 1.19→0.23 15.0→21.2\n\nTable 3: Gate-only GDPO (pre→post). Complex recall\nuses 30 complex windows; F1 /latency use 30 streams;\nspeaker counting uses a separate 80-example probe. External\nF1 /latency are 0.53/1.72 s; Table 4 gives full-chain mpWER.\nModel\n\nNo clue 2 s clue 4 s clue Full clue\n\nQwen3 base\nQwen3 chain\nMiniCPM base\nMiniCPM chain v1\nMing base\nMing chain\n\n33.0\n30.2\n58.5\n44.4\n100.0\n35.8\n\n31.9\n29.6\n56.1\n49.6\n80.6\n33.6\n\n33.7\n27.9\n58.7\n50.9\n83.7\n32.2\n\n31.6\n29.9\n57.0\n42.9\n89.9\n38.4\n\nTable 4: Full-chain capped mean mpWER (↓; per-sample\ncap 1; n=55 per condition/model). No-clue drop 95% CIs:\nQwen3 [−1.0, 7.2], MiniCPM [7.1, 21.9], Ming [57.1, 70.9].\nthe highest no-clue mpWER (100.0) and therefore has the\nmost headroom.\nMiniCPM exposes a conflict: a heavy transcription reward\nsharply lowers complex-gate recall. Table 4 reports this v1\ncheckpoint. In balanced v2, we set w = (1, 2, 2) in Equation (4) and condition transcription credit on a correct gate\nusing Equation (6). Complex-gate recall recovers above baseline while no-clue mpWER becomes 55.5 (supplement).\nHeavy training can cause gate collapse: the model reports\ncomplex for almost every clean stream (clean-stream accuracy 0, 0, and 0.11). A forced-choice probe shows a median\ncomplex probability of 1.00 for both stream types, so changing a soft threshold cannot help. At this extreme, a logit gap\nnear 20 drives both the per-token gradient p(1−p), with p\nthe gate probability, and the group advantage toward zero.\nA short supervised step provides a strong gradient in this\nstate. After 90 steps, clean-stream gate accuracy returns to\n1.0 for all models; macro-averaged mixed-stream mpWER\nalso decreases (supplement). The runs in Table 4 stop before\nthis regime; the collapse appears only under heavier training.\nGate-only GDPO predicts clue use and timing, measured\nby recall, timeline F1 , and latency; speaker counting uses\na separate probe. Its ε-mixture keeps both actions possible\nand reduces zero-variance groups. Full-chain GDPO jointly\ntrains gate and transcript, with mpWER scoring only the\ntranscript. It separately normalizes format, gate, and transcript rewards, and balanced v2 blocks transcript credit after\na wrong gate.\nAGSC adds 113–157 prompt tokens, but audio processing dominates inference. Wall-clock overhead is 0.03–0.21 s\n(2.9–11.9%) except for Ming on overlap plus noise, where\ntask completion produces an apparent 69% increase; see the\nsupplement for the protocol. Clues are built offline in 1.56 s\nand can be cached. No-clue evaluation uses the plain prompt\nwithout clue construction or clue tokens.\n\nTwo deployment paths follow. Streaming builds a clue\nfrom prefix audio and uses the learned gate to decide when\nto inject it, paying clue-building and prompt overhead. The\nno-clue path uses the plain audio prompt without the clue\nbuilder. Gate metrics evaluate the first path; no-clue mpWER\nevaluates the second.\n\nDiscussion\nOur hypothesis is simple: some complex-scene failures reflect an underused capability, not an absent one. AGSC tries\nto draw out that capability without turning its clues into\ntextual shortcuts. During SFT, an incomplete target window,\ncondition tag, or candidate list acts as a temporary trigger. We\ncall the lower error that remains after removing the trigger retained activation. Answer-overlap screening limits copying;\nthe silent-audio control measures performance after silencing the audio.\nEach check answers a different question: context-on evaluation asks whether a clue helps; silence asks whether it\ncan replace audio; no-clue evaluation asks whether behavior\npersists after scaffold removal; and streaming metrics ask\nwhether assistance is timed correctly. No check substitutes\nfor another. We use internalization for post-training persistence without clues.\nSFT targets persistence after scaffold removal; GDPO separates gate-only timing from full-chain gate-plus-transcript\ntraining under separately normalized rewards.\nHow useful a clue is depends on the scene and on how\nmuch prefix audio was available to build it. A wordless window can narrow target location, while Figure 4 shows nonmonotonic, model-specific changes as prefix evidence grows.\nTable 2 also shows that an unconditional clue after training\nneed not beat the no-clue path. There is no universal prompt.\nOur predefined templates match clue type to the diagnosed\nambiguity, and the gate limits when a clue enters.\nTogether, the results separate what persists without runtime help (no-clue mpWER), when a clue should enter (gate\nmetrics), and transcript accuracy under joint training (fullchain mpWER). Runtime clues remain in the streaming path,\nkeeping assistance optional and failures visible.\n\nConclusion\nThis paper turns a tempting shortcut into a design principle.\nAnswer-bearing context can let a model score without listening; we call this perception bypass and expose it by silencing audio. That test anchors three contributions: AGSC uses\nanswer-incomplete scaffolds to guide listening while limiting direct copying; CSB evaluates them with explicit shortcut\ncontrols; and internalization is judged by whether gains persist after clues are removed. After SFT, no-clue capped mean\nmpWER on overlap plus noise falls from 25%–71% to 9%–\n15%, providing operational evidence of internalization. For\nstreaming, gate-only GDPO learns whether and when to use\na clue; full-chain GDPO jointly trains the gate and speakerattributed transcript. Future work will extend scene-matched\nclues to new scene types and test the learned gate under domain shift. The deeper principle is simple: context should\nscaffold perception, not replace it.\n\n\fReferences\nBredin, H. 2023. pyannote.audio 2.1 speaker diarization\npipeline: principle, benchmark, and recipe. In Proceedings\nof the Annual Conference of the International Speech Communication Association (INTERSPEECH), 1983–1987.\nCarletta, J.; Ashby, S.; Bourban, S.; Flynn, M.; Guillemot,\nM.; Hain, T.; Kadlec, J.; Karaiskos, V.; Kraaij, W.; Kronenthal, M.; Lathoud, G.; Lincoln, M.; Lisowska, A.; McCowan,\nI.; Post, W.; Reidsma, D.; and Wellner, P. 2006. The AMI\nMeeting Corpus: A Pre-announcement. In Machine Learning\nfor Multimodal Interaction, Second International Workshop,\nvolume 3869 of Lecture Notes in Computer Science, 28–39.\nSpringer.\nChen, C.; Hu, Y.; Yang, C.-H. H.; Siniscalchi, S. M.; Chen,\nP.-Y.; and Chng, E. S. 2023. HyPoradise: An Open Baseline for Generative Speech Recognition with Large Language\nModels. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, Datasets and Benchmarks Track (NeurIPS), volume 36,\n31665–31688.\nChu, Y.; Xu, J.; Zhou, X.; Yang, Q.; Zhang, S.; Yan, Z.;\nZhou, C.; and Zhou, J. 2023. Qwen-Audio: Advancing Universal Audio Understanding via Unified Large-Scale AudioLanguage Models. arXiv:2311.07919.\nCornell, S.; Park, T. J.; Huang, H.; Boeddeker, C.; Chang, X.;\nMaciejewski, M.; Wiesner, M. S.; Garcia, P.; and Watanabe,\nS. 2024. The CHiME-8 DASR Challenge for Generalizable and Array Agnostic Distant Automatic Speech Recognition and Diarization. In Proceedings of the International\nWorkshop on Speech Processing in Everyday Environments\n(